ors so a term among  these two to the N terms corresponds to  selecting a 1 or an X from each of the N  factor so if I started off for example  by selecting a 1 a 1 a 1 from each of  the in fact as I get the term 1 to the N  if I selected an X and X and X and X  from each of the terms I get this last  term X to the N and if I made some  arbitrary selection like I selected an X  from the first one and a 1 from the  second one no one from the second one  from the third one I'm reading this term  one from the fourth and so on and a 1  from the next and X from the next to the  last and a 1 from the last I would get  this particular term which is not the  next one that would occur in  alphabetical order but it's just an  example so that's the simple idea if you  multiply out n terms each of which is a  sum of two things you're going to wind  up with 2 to the n terms corresponding  to every possible way of selecting one  or the other of the terms of the  components from each of the N products  so what's the coefficient of x to the K  well the coefficient of x to the K is  the number of those terms among the 2 to  the N in which the power of X is K that  is in which I selected K X's and  willy-nilly n minus K ones well how many  ways are there to select K X's among  these n terms and we know the answer to  that it's n choose K it's all the ways  of choosing a subset of K items out of n  items and that's the answer  CK is simply n choose K and this is  called the binomial formula so now we  know that 1 plus X to the N is n choose  0 plus n choose 1 X and  choose two x-squared and choose K X to  the K is the general term ending with n  choose n to the X to the N so this  expression 1 plus X is called a binomial  expression and the the choose numbers  which we've seen previously as the  number of ways to choose say in this  case k out of n elements are called  binomial coefficients and this is why  they're called binomial coefficients so  if I was going to express it more  generally I didn't need it to be one I  used 1 plus X just because it was easier  to follow the structure of the formula  but if I look at X plus y to the nth  power again the coefficients are the  same it's n choose 0 but this time Y to  the N and choose one X Y to the N minus  1 what's happening now is that I'm  choosing an X or a Y instead of an X or  a 1 from each of the terms so that the  the X Y terms are always going to have a  sum of degrees that's equal to n if I  choose K X's I must have choose and  chosen n minus K wise and there it is  expressed in a more concise form using  Sigma notation that is the binomial  formula
 

